    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "### Contributing guidelines\n\n- [X] I have read [CONTRIBUTING.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md)\n- [X] I have read [CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md)\n- [X] I have updated 'mini.nvim' to latest version\n\n### Module(s)\n\nmini.files mini.icons\n\n### Description\n\nSpecifically, files titled 'README.md' and 'init.lua', instead being preceded by a corresponding glyph, are preceded by a '/' (e.g. instead of 'I init.lua' it displays '/init.lua', which in turn makes mini.files behave problematically, as after editing or creating a file in the same folder, it treats '/init.lua' as a directory.\r\n\r\nI was able to mitigate this by explicitly defining glyphs in mini.icons' configuration:\r\n\r\n```lua\r\nopts = {\r\n    style = \"ascii\",\r\n    file = {\r\n        README = { glyph = \"R\" },\r\n        [\"README.md\"] = { glyph = \"R\" },\r\n        [\"README.txt\"] = { glyph = \"R\" },\r\n        [\"init.lua\"] = { glyph = \"I\" },\r\n    },\r\n},\r\n```\r\n\r\nWhat makes me think that this is an odd interaction specifically between mini.files and mini.icons is the fact that all glyphs show correctly within other plugins (e.g. fzf-lua).\n\n### Neovim version\n\n0.10.1\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\n1. Create a file with mini.files in a folder that contains an 'init.lua' and/or 'README.md'\r\n2. Synchronize changes\r\n\n\n### Expected behavior\n\n1. File is created\r\n2. Other files and folders remain intact\n\n### Actual behavior\n\n1. File is created\r\n2. Other files preceded by / get moved to a new folder with empty title (e.g. /init.lua gets moved to '  '/init.lua)\r\n",
